export const body = "<p>Chapter 01: Basic Concepts</p>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n\n<p>Here we are looking <em>Super Mario Bros.</em> and <em>Demon’s Souls Remake</em>. At first glance, it seems confusing to compare these two radically different games. But looking past the surface level, you may notice a common thread running through both of them.</p>\n\n<p>In these pictures, we see the player character progressing toward a goal; between them and their goals is a series of challenges. Between the player character and the goal is a level.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1fe45d6e2fdefc0faf25.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1fe45d6e2fdefc0faf25.webp\" width=\"839\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:502px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>What is a game level?</h2>\n\n<p>A game level can be understood as a playground that has a clear objective and advances the storyline upon completion. With that definition in mind, there are 3 things a game level has to provide for players:</p>\n\n<p><strong>A goal</strong>: players <em>should</em> know what they’re supposed to do to progress.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Direction</strong>: players <em>should</em> know where they need to head to and what they can interact with to get there.</p>\n\n<p><strong>An enjoyable play experience</strong>: players <em>should</em> be invested both mechanically and narratively as they progress toward the goal.</p>\n\n<p>Here, we emphasize <em>should</em> because some games intentionally go against the rules to create unique experiences.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/26eb8649ab03d318f86c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/26eb8649ab03d318f86c.webp\" width=\"969\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>What makes a level?</h2>\n\n<p>A game level has 4 main building blocks.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Story </strong>translates into emotional investment, keeping the player immersed in the game world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Design </strong>primarily relates to the play experience: the challenges and emotions of a level.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Art</strong><strong> </strong>includes all visual assets such as models, animation, and VFX.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Technology</strong> is the connective tissue binding the other building blocks together.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f7ab2e343e99d7ff73c6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f7ab2e343e99d7ff73c6.webp\" width=\"1032\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Who makes the level?</h2>\n\n<p>The main stakeholders of a level are <strong>the</strong> <strong>level designer</strong>, <strong>the</strong> <strong>concept artist</strong>, and <strong>the</strong> <strong>level artist</strong>. Of course, they’re not the only people working on the level. There are also the game director, the writer, the lighting artist, and the VFX artist, for example. But these 3 players are the most influential ones.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0b1a923d34625b8b5063.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0b1a923d34625b8b5063.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"327\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6be77c212de28cc7031a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6be77c212de28cc7031a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"557\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>What is level design?</h2>\n\n<p>Level designers map out the playgrounds for their games. They establish the direction and beats of their levels.</p>\n\n<p>You can liken them to urban planners. Both of them build spaces that people can explore at will, but there are hints in the environment to aid navigation.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3aee812155ef81b8ecb1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3aee812155ef81b8ecb1.webp\" width=\"598\" height=\"370\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7b0eef0437bda9dc279c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7b0eef0437bda9dc279c.webp\" width=\"375\" height=\"175\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>What is level art?</h2>\n\n<p>Level artists turn design blockouts into beautiful and meaningful spaces. They will create props based on proxy models and then use them to populate the environments. Through examining concept art, level artists can grasp the intended mood and tone, and generate a callout sheet.</p>\n\n<p>A callout sheet contains information about the materials and objects in a level.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f438de80782663b001e9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f438de80782663b001e9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"585\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/15f1e3e8fa26ae5224ba.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/15f1e3e8fa26ae5224ba.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"545\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> — From blockout to final</strong></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/48ce33a4924df2c0f18d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/48ce33a4924df2c0f18d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"418\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b18ff21d1bbf5886c10e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b18ff21d1bbf5886c10e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"497\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2669382609f284977846.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2669382609f284977846.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"509\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/aed11a354f0d50203f3c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/aed11a354f0d50203f3c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"424\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>The importance of level art</h2>\n\n<p>Here, we have <em>The Last of Us 1 </em>and <em>2</em>. In the top picture, you can clearly see how the rectangular props are conveniently placed for cover. Although the area functions well as a gameplay space, it doesn’t feel believable.</p>\n\n<p>The sequel improves significantly in this aspect. There are more types of props for cover, and they are placed organically within the levels (partly thanks to the more expansive environments). The result is something that plays well and feels believable at the same time.</p>\n\n<p>As a level artist, your main responsibility is to ensure game environments feel believable and meaningful. To achieve that goals, you will need to learn some skills.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/91fa3a13c6782935f5ba.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/91fa3a13c6782935f5ba.webp\" width=\"881\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:527px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>To become a level artist</h2>\n\n<h3>Hard skills</h3>\n\n<p>A level artist should have strong understanding in these areas:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Briefing</strong>: able to analyze, interpret, and generate briefs</li><li><strong>Architecture</strong>: able to construct <em>believable </em>buildings and structures</li><li><strong>Infrastructure</strong>: able to create <em>believable</em> man-made environments</li><li><strong>Nature</strong>: able to fabricate <em>believable</em> natural environments</li><li><strong>Prop </strong><strong>making</strong>: able to produce high-quality props</li><li><strong>Visual composition</strong>: able to populate the level with artistic intent</li><li><strong>Engine</strong>: able to utilize the engine toolset and optimize your level</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/be5141151946d146ed46.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/be5141151946d146ed46.webp\" width=\"766\" height=\"923\" style=\"--source-width:490px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Soft skills</h3>\n\n<p>Level artists never work alone. Not only do they collaborate they with level designers but they also work with art directors, concept artists, environment artists, lighters, and even animators at times.</p>\n\n<p>Because of that, <strong>communication</strong> and <strong>teamwork</strong> are two very important soft skills for every level artist to master.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/02739e5b7d4ee2e146e3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/02739e5b7d4ee2e146e3.webp\" width=\"838\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:501px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>The language of video games</h2>\n\n<p>Although every game is different, there is a general way in which games communicate their interactivity with players. The language of video games.</p>\n\n<p>Unfortunately, there is no better way to learn this language than to play a lot of games. Here, I will provide you with some examples, but I highly encourage you to do further research yourself.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a0dbf0c86c06cc592214.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a0dbf0c86c06cc592214.webp\" width=\"970\" height=\"852\" style=\"--source-width:484px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Regardless of how realistic games look, they always “gamify” some aspects to convey gameplay information to players. A classic example is the explosive red barrel. You see it and you know it will explode.</p>\n\n<p>The reason why developers gamify these things is to <strong>remove guesswork</strong>. For example, in a game like <em>The Last of Us</em>, there are many doors in the environments, so the developers need to help players distinguish between interactive and non-interactive doors.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/37231824bddc1c42b29a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/37231824bddc1c42b29a.webp\" width=\"813\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:486px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Navigation is one area that developers have to <strong>create</strong> <strong>a consistent visual language</strong>. The <em>Uncharted</em> series uses plenty of visual cues to aid navigation. In this specific instant, we see climbable edges sculpted with distinct shapes and highlighted by contrasting colors. These two elements combined helps the player understand what they should do.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/40b080bcd278a1e632af.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/40b080bcd278a1e632af.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"321\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Likewise, the series has been defaulting to yellow for interactive and guiding elements since <em>Resident Evil 7</em>. Yellow is the brightest color next to white, so using it in games with dark environments like these is understandable.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/18713b2cdd7446d5c117.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/18713b2cdd7446d5c117.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"281\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>In summary</h2>\n\n<p>A level is a gameplay space with an objective and a story told not only through dialogue but also visuals. Level art is, therefore, a crossroad between art and design. To become a level artist, you must learn a variety of hard skills and soft skills, and train yourself to become fluent with the language of video games.</p>\n\n<p>If these pre-requisites are already in your hands, then you’re off for a good start. If not, it’s never too late to start learning. And learn we shall!</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f5be62de3aa040be5aac.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f5be62de3aa040be5aac.webp\" width=\"712\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/02739e5b7d4ee2e146e3.webp", "assets/library-inline/0b1a923d34625b8b5063.webp", "assets/library-inline/15f1e3e8fa26ae5224ba.webp", "assets/library-inline/18713b2cdd7446d5c117.webp", "assets/library-inline/1fe45d6e2fdefc0faf25.webp", "assets/library-inline/2669382609f284977846.webp", "assets/library-inline/26eb8649ab03d318f86c.webp", "assets/library-inline/37231824bddc1c42b29a.webp", "assets/library-inline/3aee812155ef81b8ecb1.webp", "assets/library-inline/40b080bcd278a1e632af.webp", "assets/library-inline/48ce33a4924df2c0f18d.webp", "assets/library-inline/6be77c212de28cc7031a.webp", "assets/library-inline/7b0eef0437bda9dc279c.webp", "assets/library-inline/91fa3a13c6782935f5ba.webp", "assets/library-inline/a0dbf0c86c06cc592214.webp", "assets/library-inline/aed11a354f0d50203f3c.webp", "assets/library-inline/b18ff21d1bbf5886c10e.webp", "assets/library-inline/be5141151946d146ed46.webp", "assets/library-inline/f438de80782663b001e9.webp", "assets/library-inline/f5be62de3aa040be5aac.webp", "assets/library-inline/f7ab2e343e99d7ff73c6.webp"];
